22:18:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Yes—though it helps to avoid assuming disinterest is simply a lack of motivation. Prison work programs can feel unappealing when they are low-paid, repetitive, unrelated to a person’s goals, unavailable to people with certain classifications, or offer little clear benefit after release. Some people may also be dealing with stress, trauma, mental-health needs, distrust of institutions, or immediate concerns such as family and legal issues. Approaches that often work better include: - **Ask incarcerated people what they want.** Short surveys, listening sessions, or peer representatives can reveal whether they want trades, technology, creative work, entrepreneurship, education, or jobs connected to local labor demand. - **Make the work genuinely useful after release.** Industry-recognized certifications, portfolios, apprenticeships, union or employer connections, and help translating prison experience into a résumé can make participation feel worthwhile. - **Offer real choice and progression.** Clear paths from entry-level tasks to training, leadership, credentials, and better-paid roles create a sense of agency. - **Use fair incentives.** Meaningful wages, savings accounts for reentry, sentence-credit where legally available, and practical benefits such as preferred program access can matter—while avoiding coercion. - **Connect programs to actual jobs.** Partnerships with employers willing to hire people with records are especially important. Motivation understandably drops if people believe the credential will not overcome hiring barriers. - **Build trust and respect.** Staff and volunteers who are consistent, nonjudgmental, and transparent about what a program can and cannot provide may be more motivating than pressure or lectures. - **Address barriers alongside work.** Literacy support, disability accommodations, mental-health care, transportation plans, IDs, housing support, and interview preparation can determine whether work training is realistic. Your cousin may have the most impact by being curious: “What would make this opportunity worth your time?” and then helping bring those answers to program staff. Programs tend to succeed when they treat participants as partners with goals—not as people who just need to be persuaded to work.
