22:19:09 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
22:19:09 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=1320
22:19:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Having basic needs guaranteed can reduce the immediate pressure to work, but it does not automatically make people “lazy.” Most people—including students, retirees, people on disability benefits, and employees with secure salaries—still respond to purpose, autonomy, respect, social recognition, and meaningful rewards, not just survival pressure. In prison, the issue is often less “they have food and housing” than whether the work has a credible payoff. If an assignment is low-paid, compulsory-feeling, monotonous, and unlikely to help someone get a job after release, doing the bare minimum may be a predictable response. That is basic human behavior too. A workable program can acknowledge both realities: - Don’t expect gratitude for basic confinement conditions. - Set clear work standards and apply rules consistently. - Tie sustained effort to concrete, lawful benefits: better assignments, certifications, wages/savings, references, educational access, or reentry support. - Give participants some choice and a visible route to advancement. - Ensure the program does not exploit labor or promise outcomes it cannot deliver. That is not excusing refusal or poor performance. It is designing incentives around how people actually behave, while holding individuals accountable for their own choices.
