22:19:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Confinement can reduce autonomy, initiative, and opportunities to practice ordinary responsibility—so there is a real point there: when people have little control over their day and little connection between effort and outcome, they may become passive or disengaged. But comparing incarcerated people to zoo animals is not a useful or fair way to frame it. People in prison remain individuals with goals, skills, families, health needs, and the ability to make choices. The relevant lesson is about the effects of an institution, not a claim that prisoners are naturally less human or inherently incapable of responsibility. If the goal is participation in work, the practical implication is simple: avoid programs that create learned passivity. Give clear standards, real responsibilities, credible rewards, skill development, and a believable link between good performance and better prospects after release. That can coexist with supervision and consequences for people who choose not to participate.
