22:20:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A documentary narrator may be describing a real pattern in the prison or program they filmed, especially if jobs are unpopular, poorly paid, or heavily controlled. But documentaries also condense a complicated institution into a story, and “most inmates avoid labor whenever they can” is a broad claim that should be supported by representative data, not just narration and memorable scenes. It is reasonable to say that many prison work assignments require close supervision and that some participants will avoid effort when they can. It is not reliable to turn that into “incarcerated people are inherently lazy.” For your cousin’s situation, the actionable conclusion remains the same: use consistent oversight, clear and fair expectations, concrete incentives, and work that builds a credible path to employment after release. That handles low effort whether its cause is indifference, poor program design, or individual choice.
