22:21:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Some people who work in prisons every day do report frequent shirking, refusal, and low engagement in certain work assignments. That operational perspective matters, and it would be a mistake to dismiss it. But it is not true that only outsiders disagree with the blanket conclusion. Correctional staff, prison educators, vocational instructors, reentry workers, and formerly incarcerated people often distinguish between: - a work program where many people do the minimum, and - a claim that incarceration proves someone has a permanently lazy character. Those are different claims. A person can be difficult to motivate in a coercive, low-reward environment and still work reliably in a job that offers pay, dignity, responsibility, and a realistic future benefit. For practical management, your cousin does not need to presume either saintliness or laziness. She can assume engagement will vary, insist on standards, notice who follows through, and support programs that make effort worthwhile.
