22:23:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Some people may have avoided steady lawful work before incarceration, just as some people outside prison do. But it is not reasonable to assume that is why “a lot” of people are incarcerated. People end up in prison for many different reasons: violence, addiction, untreated mental illness, impulsive decisions, coercion, unstable housing, limited education, criminal networks, financial desperation, and sometimes offenses committed while they were employed or caring for families. Many incarcerated people did work before prison, often in unstable, low-wage, seasonal, or informal jobs. So “they were too lazy to work honestly” is one possible explanation for an individual only if their own history supports it—not a reliable explanation for incarceration as a group. For a work program, the useful standard is still concrete: evaluate whether each person shows up, learns, follows rules, and performs over time. If someone repeatedly refuses opportunities without a legitimate barrier, address that behavior. But don’t treat a conviction as evidence that the person is unwilling to work.
